  {
    "episode_id": 78079,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78079.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 19,
    "turn_text": "I think you got to go back 62 years when I started in the state legislature because when you spend 16 years in the state's legislature, then four years in the six years in the US House, then you come to the Senate There's not a lot that's different. You kind of evolve into being a senator. It's not something I planned when I was in grade school. It's just my political career kind of evolved from one position to the other to the other. But I think that what I learned between the Senate and the House that I would tell people, run for the Senate instead of for the House, because first of all, you got to get permission from some committee chairman to speak in the House. And you might only get two or three minutes You got two year terms to get your job done, and if you're in the minority, you don't really count. Whereas in the United States Senate, you got six years to get done what you want to get done. If you get the floor, you can speak for as long as you want to speak. I was always in the minority in the house. I came to the Senate. as the majority, and it's always better to be in the majority than it is in the minority.",
