  {
    "episode_id": 78079,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/8/78079.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "Well, it's probably a little bit different for me than it was for about 97 other senators because I was President Rotem at the time. I'm not now because we're in the minority. I have the first 38 years. I never had any police protection. But when you become President Rotem, you have not secret service, but Capitol Police that are with you constantly 24 hours a day. So I had just taken the podium After the vice president left and I'm not sure I knew why he left I didn't a few minutes and all I got to do was say that the Senate will stand at ease until the fall of the gavel and then my entourage got me out of there so it wasn't until I got to a safe place about 10 minutes away. that I saw on television what was happening. So unlike my 97 other colleagues that had to kind of stay in the center chamber for, I don't know, minutes or maybe 15 minutes, maybe half hour, I don't know how long until somebody thought it was safe to leave and then they left and got down in the tunnels as fast as they could. But I wasn't aware of that at the time. I wasn't even a, I'm not sure I was aware of what was happening until I got to the safe place and sought on television. And I was abhorred by what",
